   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to provide that interests or rights acquired in real property for the preservation of agricultural lands in the county of Suffolk shall permit the use of such real property for a farm operation",
    "basePrintNo": "S6039",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6039-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Suffolk County",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6039\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to provide that interests or rights acquired\nin real property for the preservation of agricultural lands in the\ncounty of Suffolk shall permit the use of such real property for a\nfarm operation\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo clarify that interests and rights in real property acquired for the\npreservation of open lands actually used in agricultural production in\nSuffolk County may be used as a farm operation by defining the meaning\nof the term \"farm operation\".\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1. Provides that in the County of Suffolk, all interests or\nrights in real property acquired for the preservation of agricultural\nlands, pursuant to Section 247 of the General Municipal Law, shall\npermit the use of such property as a farm operation. For the purposes\nof this act, a \"farm operation\" shall have the same meaning a provided\nfor in Section 301 of the Agriculture and Markets Law\n\nSection 2. Authorizes the County of Suffolk to adopt a local law to\nadminister the interests or rights in real property acquired for the\npreservation of agricultural lands, pursuant to Section 247 of the\nGeneral Municipal Law. Such local law may include but is not limited\nto requiring a permit for uses, buildings and structures on such real\nproperty, consistent with the definition of a farm operation. The\ngranting of a permit for uses, buildings, and structures consistent\nwith a farm operation shall not constitute alienation of any interest\nor rights in real property acquired for the preservation of\nagricultural lands, pursuant to section 247 of the general municipal\nlaw. Such local law shall be subject to and consistent with the\nprovisions of section 308 of the agriculture and markets law\nprotecting the right to farm.\n\nSection 3. This act shall be applicable to all interests or rights in\nreal property acquired by the county of Suffolk for the preservation\nof agricultural lands, pursuant to section 247 of the General\nMunicipal Law, now-owned or hereafter acquired.\n\nSection 4. Effective Date\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe County of Suffolk instituted the first farmland preservation\nprogram in the nation in 1974. In addition, several towns in the\ncounty have administered their own farmland preservation programs for\nmore than 35 years. Under the program, development rights are\npurchased from the owners of farmland, pursuant to Section 247 of the\nGeneral Municipal Law. Local governments buy the development rights to\nthe real property, permitting only the use of the land for bona fide\nagricultural production. Land stays in private ownership and local\ngovernment acquires non-agricultural development rights.\n\nThese rights are valued as the difference between the full market\nvalue of property for its \"highest and best use\" (full value) minus\nthe value of the agricultural rights (residual value). The owner files\nproperty covenants similar to a conservation easement limiting the use\nof the property to agricultural production.\n\nSuffolk County alone has protected more than 10,500 acres of farmland\nunder this program. The towns has protected several thousand\nadditional acquires. As a result, Suffolk County remains one of the\n\ntop agricultural counties in the state in terms of the value of\nagricultural production.\n\nIn September 2016, in the case of Long Island Pine Barrens Society, et\nal. v. Suffolk County Legislature et al, the State Supreme Court\nstruck down two county local laws which regulated the use of these\nprotected agricultural lands by authorizing the issuance of permits\nfor certain uses of protected farmland. The court found that such uses\nconstituted the alienation of public rights acquired pursuant to\nSection 247 and that such alienation could only be accomplished by a\npublic referendum.\n\nThis court decision has been interpreted as standing for the\nproposition that activities necessary for agricultural production also\nconstitute alienation. This has resulted in confusion as to what\nagricultural activities may be legally undertaken on protected\nfarmland. It has also had a chilling effect on future farmer\nparticipation in the sale of development rights to local government.\nThis chilling effect, if not reversed, will result in the unnecessary\nconversion of critical farmland to suburban development.\n\nIt has always been the clear intent of the program that the\nacquisition of development rights was not intended to prevent\ngenerally accepted, sound agricultural practices. In fact, rights to\nthese open lands were acquired not merely to keep the land open, but\nto also encourage the continuation of agriculture as a viable\nindustry, in the face of encroaching suburbia and rapidly increasing\nland values that threatened agriculture.\n\nAt the heart of this dispute is the lack of a definition as to what\nconstitutes a \"farm operation\" in the context of the farmland\npreservation program. This act defines a \"farm operation\" consistent\nwith Section 301 of the State Agriculture and Markets Law. This act\nalso makes it clear that activities related to such farm operation are\nlegally permissible on protected farmland under Section 247 of the\nGML. It also recognizes the right of local government to administer\nthose rights to balance the rights of farmers and the public. Finally,\nthe law would apply to all protected farmland acquired under Section\n247 of the GML now-owned or acquired in the future.\n\nThis act will insure that one of the most hailed and recognized\nfarmland preservation programs in the nation can continue to meet its\ngoals to protect both agricultural lands and agriculture.\n\nNothing herein, would change the fact that utilization of protected\nfarmland for non-agricultural purposes would be prohibited and that\nany action by local government permitting such uses would constitute\nalienation of public rights requiring a public referendum.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2017: New Legislation\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
